  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... First snowfall for parts of the Alleghenies with \nspotty showers & brisk conditions elsewhere through Saturday.\n\nLatest surface analyses and satellite data depict low pressure\nmoving well off to our northeast and occluding off the NJ/NY\ncoast. Any lingering showers have cleared for the moment as most\nprecipitation remains to the northeast closer to the center low,\nwith numerous pieces of upper-level energy rotating about its \naxis. \n\nOne of these pieces of energy moves through later this \nmorning, and interacts with the aforementioned occluding \nboundary offshore. This may be enough to throw some showers back\ninto northeast MD on the back side of the departing low. \nOtherwise, most areas likely stay mostly dry for much of the \nday. As flow turns more northwesterly aloft throughout the day, \nwe start to get a bit more of a connection to the moisture \nsource off the Great Lakes moving towards the Allegheny Front. \nThis will lead to another round of upslope snow shower activity \nby tonight into early Saturday. These will of course be \nmaximized at elevations above 2000 feet and in particular the \nupslope favored zones of western Garrett, western Pendleton, and\nwestern Grant counties. If travel disruptions were to \nmaterializes, expect impacts to US-219 from the MD/PA line to WV\nline, US-48 from Mount Storm, WV west to Davis, WV, and US-33 \nwest of Franklin, WV. Snow accumulations will remain on the \norder of a coating to 2\" with the greater probabilities of 1-2\" \nwith localized 3\" amounts above 3000 feet pending any \nsqualls/streamers (mainly late Friday night through Saturday \nmidday).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below normal temperatures and dry condtions are \nexpected Monday and Tuesday before a warm up occurs.\n\nHeights will start rising Monday as the amplified upper level \npatterns slowly progresses eastward. Winds should be much less \nnoticeable by Monday and Tuesday as the surface high finally reaches \nthe Mid Atlantic, although temperatures remain below normal. Given \nthe lighter winds, the subsequent nights could turn out colder in \nmany areas, with widespread near or below freezing temperatures \noutside of the urban centers and tidal shoreline. Since this will be \nmore than two weeks after the average first freeze, no frost/freeze \nheadlines will be issued.\n\nFurther upper level amplification will likely happen toward mid \nweek. While a trough or low could develop toward the southeast \ncoast, it appears that ridging will dominate the Mid Atlantic, \nmaintaining dry conditions through next week. The ridging will also \nlead to a warming trend, with high temperatures rising back into the \n60s for many areas Wednesday and especially Thursday.",
